bowshock is an independent implementation of published numerical methods.
It contains no code from FluidX3D.

Methods implemented, with references, are listed below and cited at the
point of use in the source code.

Shock tube test problem
  G. A. Sod, "A survey of several finite difference methods for systems of
  nonlinear hyperbolic conservation laws", J. Comput. Phys. 27, 1978.

Exact Riemann solver for the Euler equations
  E. F. Toro, Riemann Solvers and Numerical Methods for Fluid Dynamics,
  3rd ed., Springer, 2009, chapter 4 (also the source of the five 1D test
  problems in src/validation/toro-tests.ts).

HLLC approximate Riemann solver
  E. F. Toro, M. Spruce and W. Speares, "Restoration of the contact surface
  in the HLL-Riemann solver", Shock Waves 4, 1994.
  P. Batten, N. Clarke, C. Lambert and D. M. Causon, "On the choice of
  wavespeeds for the HLLC Riemann solver", SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 18(6), 1997.

Wave-speed estimates, and the HLL flux with them (HLLE)
  B. Einfeldt, "On Godunov-type methods for gas dynamics", SIAM J. Numer.
  Anal. 25(2), 1988. HLL as given in Toro's book above, section 10.3.

Rotated hybrid of HLL and HLLC
  H. Nishikawa and K. Kitamura, "Very simple, carbuncle-free,
  boundary-layer-resolving, rotated-hybrid Riemann solvers", J. Comput.
  Phys. 227, 2008.
  H. G. Steggles, M. G. Hoare and J. M. Pittard, MNRAS 466, 2016,
  appendix B (the HLL/HLLC form).

MUSCL reconstruction and slope limiters
  B. van Leer, "Towards the ultimate conservative difference scheme. II.
  Monotonicity and conservation combined in a second-order scheme",
  J. Comput. Phys. 14, 1974 (harmonic limiter).
  B. van Leer, "Towards the ultimate conservative difference scheme. IV.
  A new approach to numerical convection", J. Comput. Phys. 23, 1977
  (monotonized central limiter).
  B. van Leer, "Towards the ultimate conservative difference scheme. V.
  A second-order sequel to Godunov's method", J. Comput. Phys. 32, 1979.
  G. D. van Albada, B. van Leer and W. W. Roberts, "A comparative study of
  computational methods in cosmic gas dynamics", Astron. Astrophys. 108, 1982.

Strong-stability-preserving Runge-Kutta time integration
  C.-W. Shu and S. Osher, "Efficient implementation of essentially
  non-oscillatory shock-capturing schemes", J. Comput. Phys. 77, 1988.

Compensated summation of the GPU clock (TwoSum)
  D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, vol. 2: Seminumerical
  Algorithms, Addison-Wesley.

Isentropic vortex verification case
  C.-W. Shu, "Essentially non-oscillatory and weighted essentially
  non-oscillatory schemes for hyperbolic conservation laws", in Advanced
  Numerical Approximation of Nonlinear Hyperbolic Equations, Lecture Notes
  in Mathematics 1697, Springer, 1998.

Oblique shock, normal shock and Prandtl-Meyer relations
  Ames Research Staff, "Equations, Tables, and Charts for Compressible
  Flow", NACA Report 1135, 1953.

Nose cone profiles (src/geometry/nose.ts)
  Shapes and parameter conventions as defined by OpenRocket
  (core/src/main/java/info/openrocket/core/rocketcomponent/Transition.java,
  https://github.com/openrocket/openrocket), so that imported designs
  match; the formulas are reimplemented, no OpenRocket code is included.
  Haack series: W. Haack, "Geschossformen kleinsten Wellenwiderstandes",
  Bericht 139 der Lilienthal-Gesellschaft fuer Luftfahrtforschung, 1941.
  The tests check its defining slender-body property directly.

Method of manufactured solutions (src/validation/manufactured.ts)
  P. J. Roache, "Code verification by the method of manufactured
  solutions", J. Fluids Eng. 124(1), 2002.

Taylor-Maccoll cone flow (src/validation/taylor-maccoll.ts)
  G. I. Taylor and J. W. Maccoll, "The air pressure on a cone moving at
  high speeds", Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A 139, 1933.

Method of characteristics for rotational axisymmetric flow
(src/validation/characteristics.ts)
  M. Inouye, J. V. Rakich and H. Lomax, "A description of numerical
  methods and computer programs for two-dimensional and axisymmetric
  supersonic flow over blunt-nosed and flared bodies", NASA TN D-2970,
  1965.
  L. B. Callis, "An analysis of supersonic flow phenomena in conical
  nozzles by a method of characteristics", NASA TN D-3550, 1966.

Grid convergence index and oscillatory convergence
(src/verification/grid-convergence.ts)
  I. B. Celik, U. Ghia, P. J. Roache and C. J. Freitas, "Procedure for
  estimation and reporting of uncertainty due to discretization in CFD
  applications", J. Fluids Eng. editorial policy note, 2008.
  F. Stern, R. V. Wilson, H. W. Coleman and E. G. Paterson,
  "Comprehensive approach to verification and validation of CFD
  simulations, part 1", J. Fluids Eng. 123, 2001.

Shock standoff of a sphere, to place blunt-body grid boundaries
(src/grid/blunt-grid.ts)
  F. S. Billig, "Shock-wave shapes around spherical- and cylindrical-nosed
  bodies", J. Spacecraft Rockets 4, 1967.

Oklab colour space for the colour map (src/render/colormap.ts) and the 3D
view's palettes (src/render3d/quantities.ts)
  B. Ottosson, "A perceptual color space for image processing", 2020.

The 3D view (src/render3d/): iso-lines by marching squares on the dual
mesh, on a resampling by cubic convolution, simplified by the
Ramer-Douglas-Peucker algorithm
  W. E. Lorensen and H. E. Cline, "Marching cubes: a high resolution 3D
  surface construction algorithm", Computer Graphics 21(4), 1987 (the
  two-dimensional case, marching squares).
  R. G. Keys, "Cubic convolution interpolation for digital image
  processing", IEEE Trans. Acoust., Speech, Signal Process. 29(6), 1981
  (a = -0.5, the Catmull-Rom cubic).
  U. Ramer, "An iterative procedure for the polygonal approximation of
  plane curves", Computer Graphics and Image Processing 1(3), 1972;
  D. H. Douglas and T. K. Peucker, "Algorithms for the reduction of the
  number of points required to represent a digitized line or its
  caricature", Cartographica 10(2), 1973.
Its look follows FluidX3D's renders as its README and the pictures it
links show them; none of FluidX3D's code was read or used.

Standard atmosphere (src/atmosphere/us-standard-1976.ts)
  U.S. Standard Atmosphere, 1976, NOAA, NASA and USAF, Washington, D.C.,
  1976 (NOAA-S/T 76-1562, NASA-TM-X-74335), Part 1: defining constants
  and equations; tested against its Tables I and III.

